--- body ---
## Context

This is path A of the asset-machinery defect class tracked by 0278, and the
one part of it that is unambiguously worth doing.

With no `.erg-assets` stamp, three channels go silent for the same single
cause: `assetDriftWarnings` (`manifest.go:184`) returns `nil` the moment
`stamps == nil`; `corpusWarnings` therefore has nothing to report through
either `cmdCheck` or `cmdInit`'s chained post-init check; and `erg update`'s
post-swap hint (`update.go:226`) is gated on `os.Stat(manifestName)`, so it
never even asks. Demonstrated: chemin-de-voix reports `would preserve` with
no warning anywhere.

**Path A's defining property is silence, not destruction.** An unrecognized
hash already degrades safely — preserve, exit 2 — so nothing is being lost
today; the store simply cannot tell the user it has no provenance. That is
why this is separable from, and higher-value than, 0281's hash table: the
table would let such stores auto-upgrade, but not building it costs only
convenience, while leaving the silence costs the user the ability to find
out anything at all.

Split from 0281 so a safe, load-bearing fix is reviewed on its own merits
rather than alongside a change that creates a hazard of its own (0281's
path C: a wrong table entry destroys a genuine local edit).

## Relevant files

- `src/go/manifest.go:184` — `assetDriftWarnings`, the single root cause
- `src/go/manifest.go:24` — `assetDriftSignal`, the existing
  producer/consumer-shared-literal pattern the new constant follows
- `src/go/update.go:226` — the `os.Stat(manifestName)` gate on the post-swap
  hint
- `src/go/check.go:173,251` — `corpusWarnings` and `cmdCheck`
- `src/go/init.go:10-13,262` — `initAssetPaths`, and the chained post-init
  check

## Actions

1. **`assetDriftWarnings` stops returning `nil` when the manifest is
   absent.** Instead, for each `rel` in `initAssetPaths` (`.ergrc` and
   `AGENTS.md` only — the other two embedded files are `orphanAssetPaths`
   and never reach this machinery), read the on-disk file; if it exists and
   its SHA-256 differs from the currently embedded content, append one
   warning naming the asset and the new signal constant. If the file is
   absent, or matches the embedded copy exactly, emit nothing for it. That
   gate preserves the existing property the doc comment states: a store that
   never touched erg's asset management is never nagged.
2. **New constant beside `assetDriftSignal`** — `assetStamplessSignal`, a
   stable substring, following the same shared-literal pattern. Wording
   should point at the remedy, e.g. `"NOTE %s: no .erg-assets stamp --
   cannot tell whether this is a clean upgrade or a local edit; run
   'erg init' to find out (existing edits are never overwritten without
   --force)"`.
3. **`update.go` shares the detection instead of re-deciding it.** Drop the
   `os.Stat(manifestName)` pre-check (or loosen it to "ticketDir exists") so
   the re-exec'd `erg check` always runs after a binary swap, then match the
   combined output against *either* signal, with two distinct follow-ups —
   "run erg init to refresh" for drift, "run erg init to establish
   provenance" for stampless.

Fixing `assetDriftWarnings` is the root-cause change: it is the one function
`corpusWarnings` calls, and `corpusWarnings` feeds both `cmdCheck` and
`cmdInit`. Do not patch the three channels separately.

## Test

**Red step** — `TestAssetDriftWarningsReportsStamplessDivergence` in
`src/go/manifest_test.go`:

- `t.TempDir()` with `tickets/.ergrc` holding content that is **not** the
  current embedded content (follow the fixture idiom in
  `TestInstallAssetsHistoricalUpgrade`), and deliberately **no**
  `.erg-assets`.
- Call `assetDriftWarnings(dir)` directly.
- Assert on **message content**, not count or exit code — path A's property
  is silence, and the return value is `nil`-ish both before and after a
  broken fix, so only a content assertion can see it.

Today this fails: `assetDriftWarnings` returns `nil` as soon as
`stamps == nil`, so the substring is never found.

**Required positive control, in the same test** — `.ergrc` on disk matching
the embedded content exactly, still no manifest, must produce **zero**
stampless warnings. Without this arm, an implementation that warns
unconditionally on "no manifest" passes the red step and proves nothing
about gating on real divergence.

**Integration arm** — extend `tests/test_check.sh` (already in
`TEST_SUITES`, no new suite needed) with a fixture whose `tickets/.ergrc` is
old-shipped content and has no `.erg-assets`, asserting `erg check`'s stderr
carries the same substring. This proves the fix reaches the CLI channel a
human or agent actually reads; the unit test is still the one to write and
watch fail first.

## Dependencies

`Blocked-by: 0279`, which changes `isCleanUpgrade`'s signature and extends
the manifest reader. This ticket never calls `isCleanUpgrade` and only needs
"does a stamp exist", so it is robust to 0279's exact shape: if 0279 wraps
the manifest return in a struct rather than a bare map, Action 1's
nil-check becomes a field check — mechanical, not a redesign. A compile
break at `assetDriftWarnings`'s existing `readManifestFile` call site is
expected and fixed at rebase time, not a reason to resequence.

## Verification

- [ ] the red test fails before the change and passes after
- [ ] the same-content positive control passes both before and after
- [ ] a stampless store reports its condition through `erg check`
- [ ] a stampless store reports its condition after `erg update`
- [ ] a store whose assets match the embedded copy exactly is still silent

## Invariants

- A store that never adopted erg's asset management is never nagged — the
  warning gates on real divergence, not on the absence of a manifest.
- No behaviour change to the overwrite/preserve decision itself. This ticket
  makes a condition *visible*; it does not change what `erg init` does about
  it.
- `erg init` on a pristine current asset stays a silent no-op.

## Exit criteria

- A store with no provenance manifest is told so, on every channel that
  would have reported drift — `erg check`, `erg init`'s chained check, and
  `erg update`'s post-swap hint
- The message names the remedy
- The three channels share one detection path rather than three gates

## Related

- 0278 — tracker; this is path A, the demonstrated-silent one
- 0279 — lands first; this ticket is `Blocked-by` it
- 0281 — the generated hash table, now `Blocked-by` this ticket. It would
  let a stampless store at a known shipped hash auto-upgrade; this ticket
  only makes the condition visible. The table is optional and carries its
  own hazard; visibility is not.

## Feasibility (Phase 4, 2026-09-16)

All symbols this plan names were verified present. One correction for
whoever executes it:

The gate is at `update.go:226` as stated, but it is spelled
`os.Stat(filepath.Join(ticketDir, manifestName))`, not the shorthand
`os.Stat(manifestName)` the Actions section uses. The line number is exact;
only the expression is abbreviated.

More importantly, the four-line comment directly above it encodes the very
premise this ticket overturns:

> Only attempted when a manifest exists: without one there is nothing to
> compare and no hint is warranted.

That is false, and it is the reasoning that produced the silence. Without a
manifest there *is* something to compare -- on-disk content against the
embedded copy -- which is exactly what Action 1 adds. **Rewrite that comment
as part of the change.** Leaving it in place would leave the next reader the
same wrong premise, and a future refactor would re-derive the same gate from
it.
